WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.160
Today, I’m going to be looking at a randomly&nbsp;
selected deck from one of my Patrons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:09.440
with this deck coming from Jovism. His deck’s&nbsp;
commander pairing is Erinis, Gloom Stalker with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:13.360
a background of Street Urchin, and the first&nbsp;
thing I note about this pairing is that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:18.520
feels like two different commanders much more so&nbsp;
than most other background pairings. You know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:22.640
you’ve got a bunch of cases where the background&nbsp;
basically acts as an enhancement of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:29.640
commander—Baeloth but big, Burakos but he draws&nbsp;
some extra cards, Wilson but he’s a demon lord,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:35.520
and so on. By contrast, Street Urchin pairs with&nbsp;
Erinis’ deathtouch to turn into a removal engine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:40.680
so the command zone effectively ends up offering&nbsp;
two entirely separate things—land recursion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:44.880
and removal. Let’s start by thinking about&nbsp;
the land recursion part—the most simple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:50.160
usage here is using the ability to ramp with&nbsp;
evolving wilds type lands and harrow effects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:55.000
and indeed the deck is doing a good amount of&nbsp;
this. It also contains several different lands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:58.920
with interesting sacrifice abilities,&nbsp;
as well as four artifact lands to fuel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:03.080
Street Urchin. For the removal part,&nbsp;
the deck is running lots of artifacts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:07.760
and creatures it’s okay with sacrificing,&nbsp;
as well as cards that generate tokens.

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:12.360
With these two things—land recursion and&nbsp;
removal—in its pocket, the deck is looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:16.520
to generate advantage in cards and mana over the&nbsp;
course of the game while keeping opponents in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:20.480
check via its built-in creature removal as well&nbsp;
as a number of removal spells for artifacts and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:25.120
enchantments. Once the board state is under&nbsp;
control and it has built up enough resources,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:28.800
the deck then looks to win either byy&nbsp;
swinging with a board of big creatures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:33.960
or by playing a buff equipment and hitting its&nbsp;
blocker-less opponents with Erinis a few times.

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:39.040
Having laid out my understanding of what the deck&nbsp;
is trying to do, my next question is how is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:43.800
deck doing at accomplishing its goals? First&nbsp;
of all, the removal engine in the command zone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:48.920
is pretty fearsome—at any point in time you can&nbsp;
pay 1 and sacrifice an artifact or creature to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:55.000
kill an opposing creature. The deck packs 19 total&nbsp;
cards that generate creature or artifact tokens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:59.480
and 11 other artifacts and creatures that the&nbsp;
deck is either indifferent about sacrificing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:04.400
or actively wants to sacrifice. I want to&nbsp;
highlight a couple sac-fodder cards I find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:09.480
particularly neat—Scion of Calamity is a nice&nbsp;
creature with myriad, and the tokens it creates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:13.600
can either be sacrificed in response to their&nbsp;
damage triggers, when they’ve already gotten all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:18.520
of their value, or before blockers are declared,&nbsp;
so that the deck can clear a path for Erinis to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:24.760
dish out some commander damage. Another fun one is&nbsp;
Humble Defector—activate, sacrifice in response,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:30.640
get them cards. Finally, Treasure Vault is a cool&nbsp;
option for resource-building—it can be sacrificed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:35.320
on an opponent’s endstep and brought back each&nbsp;
combat step, allowing the deck to accumulate mana&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:39.520
and sacrificeable artifacts if the game is in a&nbsp;
lull and it doesn’t quite have what it needs to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:45.040
win yet. Overall, the suite of sacrificeables&nbsp;
that the deck has seems more than adequate.

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:51.200
The number of ways to get lands into the graveyard&nbsp;
is also pretty solid—8 slow fetches, 7 other sac&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:56.960
lands, 10 discard effects, 4 artifact lands that&nbsp;
can get sacrificed to Street Urchin, 3 Harrow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:03.120
effects, and a few other cards. The deck also runs&nbsp;
quite a lot of draw overall, including a whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:08.560
bunch of filtering, some more generic choices, and&nbsp;
a handful of things such as Keen Sense that pair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:13.280
nicely with the commander. This draw, paired with&nbsp;
the deck’s healthy suites of cards supporting its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:19.200
command zone, lead to the deck being able to jump&nbsp;
into its land-recursion-and-removal-based gameplan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:24.240
with a high degree of consistency. The deck’s&nbsp;
ramp suite is particularly interesting to me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:28.840
in the sense that the only ramp card in the deck&nbsp;
that isn't specifically supporting the command&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:34.560
zone is Sol Ring. The deck could run dorks&nbsp;
to boost out Erinis a turn earlier or rocks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:39.240
to boost the deck’s overall mana count going into&nbsp;
the midgame, but these options would eat up slots&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:44.440
that could go to making sure the deck has lands in&nbsp;
yard and stuff to sacrifice on board. The result&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:49.720
of this choice is that the deck operates with&nbsp;
a very consistent curve—Erinis played turn 3,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:55.240
Street Urchin presumably played turn 2 or 4, and&nbsp;
whatever other utility plays needed to set up the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.240 --> 00:03:59.680
deck’s engines on other turns, with Erinis&nbsp;
ramping a land on the combat step of turn 4.

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:03.720
As far as winning the game goes, the&nbsp;
deck has a few buff equipments that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:08.040
can let the commander kill opponents in&nbsp;
2-3 hits, a few big landfall creatures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:13.360
and a handful of other sizable creatures. On the&nbsp;
surface, this doesn’t seem like very much—can this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:18.480
selection of stuff really win the game? Well I&nbsp;
haven’t played games with the deck myself so I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:23.960
obviously don’t know for sure, but it follows&nbsp;
a principle I laid out in my wincons video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:29.000
which is that control decks can win games with&nbsp;
less powerful finishers than midrange decks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:33.520
simply because they generally have more time to&nbsp;
work with and less on-board resistance to worry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:38.560
about. Once Gruul Control gets the game into a&nbsp;
soft lock, where major opposing threats are all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:43.400
under control, hitting opponents three times each&nbsp;
with an adventuring Erinis is a win plan that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:48.560
could absolutely see being viable. Playing more&nbsp;
powerful cards to win is certainly an option,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:53.760
but I could totally see an argument that if&nbsp;
the deck doesn’t need them, why play them?

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:58.240
Another area of the deck I can comment on&nbsp;
is the manabase, and with 21 green symbols&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:03.840
and 18 red symbols within the lands, I’d&nbsp;
call the manabase not great but passable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:07.760
and it’s certainly somewhat helped by the&nbsp;
deck having 9 instances of low-cost filtering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:13.160
available at single red, as well as land recursion&nbsp;
in the command zone for single green. Finally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:18.080
I like that the deck contains several protection&nbsp;
spells since it’s so reliant on the command zone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:22.840
and I think this problem is also somewhat&nbsp;
helped by Erinis’ cheap cost and built-in ramp,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.840 --> 00:05:28.560
which make needing to replay them once or twice&nbsp;
not a big deal. The reactive part of the command&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:34.720
zone package, the removal, is also usable any&nbsp;
time both Erinis and Street Urchin are in play,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:38.920
meaning that opponents will struggle to keep this&nbsp;
removal engine offline for long unless they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.920 --> 00:05:43.840
willing to throw a lot of pieces of removal at&nbsp;
the commander. Erinis is still naturally going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:47.640
to be a weak point due to the deck being&nbsp;
heavily built around them being in play,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:52.280
but due to the factors I mentioned I don’t&nbsp;
think it’s a crippling problem or anything.

00:05:52.280 --> 00:05:56.480
Okay, I’ve gone through the stuff that I think&nbsp;
the deck either does well or does acceptably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.480 --> 00:06:01.480
at. If I had to sum up the biggest issue I&nbsp;
see with this deck, it’s actually mostly a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.480 --> 00:06:07.640
social one. This is a decidedly mid-power deck&nbsp;
in terms of its speed and how it wins the game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.640 --> 00:06:12.520
but for a mid-power deck it’s brutally effective&nbsp;
at killing creatures, and there will naturally be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:16.600
players at that power who aren’t thrilled to have&nbsp;
their board kept empty of meaningful threats the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:21.560
whole game. So why not make the deck more powerful&nbsp;
to have it play at a level with more tuned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.560 --> 00:06:26.160
cutthroat decks? The issue is that there&nbsp;
are several factors about this deck that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:31.040
make increasing the power level difficult, and&nbsp;
to demonstrate this I’m going to explore a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:36.120
different potential improvement routes for the&nbsp;
deck and talk about where I think they fall short.

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:41.200
For one, what if we increase the speed of the&nbsp;
deck? Add some one mana dorks, or replace some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:46.600
Evolving Wilds-type cards with actual fetchlands?&nbsp;
As I mentioned earlier, the issue with adding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.600 --> 00:06:51.600
faster ramp is that the deck’s choice not to run&nbsp;
ramp that doesn’t support the command zone is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:56.400
part of what lets it jump into its core gameplan&nbsp;
with a high degree of consistency. If you start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.400 --> 00:07:01.880
replacing Harrow effects and treasure generators&nbsp;
with ramp that lets you get out Erinis on turn 2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.880 --> 00:07:06.440
you’ll get Erinis triggers earlier, but you’ll&nbsp;
on average have fewer lands in the graveyard and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.440 --> 00:07:12.120
fewer tokens to sacrifice, negating some of that&nbsp;
increase in speed. I think ultimately efforts to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.120 --> 00:07:16.720
improve the deck’s ramp suite would end up a&nbsp;
wash, giving it occasionally more explosive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:22.320
games at the cost of doing what it wants&nbsp;
to do less consistently. Actual fetchlands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:27.680
on the other hand, would likely make the deck&nbsp;
faster, but they alone won’t make the deck that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:33.840
much better, and are also pretty pricey. Another&nbsp;
improvement route would be adding more protection,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:39.120
since the deck relies heavily on its command zone&nbsp;
and thus has a pretty big point of failure. And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:43.280
wouldn’t mind a couple more protective cards,&nbsp;
but this is once again a bit tricky due to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.280 --> 00:07:47.640
deck wanting to have as many slots as possible&nbsp;
devoted to making sure its command zone is able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.640 --> 00:07:53.240
to do what it wants to do—additional protection&nbsp;
comes at the cost of the speed and consistency of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.240 --> 00:07:58.760
the core gameplan. The deck’s colors are another&nbsp;
challenge—a lack of white or blue means that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.760 --> 00:08:03.200
deck will struggle to deal with certain types&nbsp;
of removal, and this color pairing additionally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:07.880
limits the amount of stack interaction the&nbsp;
deck is able to run to prevent opposing wins.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.880 --> 00:08:12.040
Destroying some key creatures and artifacts&nbsp;
might be able to slow down opposing decks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.040 --> 00:08:16.120
but this is the sort of deck that will really&nbsp;
struggle to contain more explosive wins if it ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.120 --> 00:08:22.440
falls a bit behind, making decks that win with&nbsp;
combos and noncreature means a tricky matchup.

00:08:22.440 --> 00:08:27.920
This brings me to a third possible way to improve&nbsp;
the deck—improving its finisher suite. One option&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:32.480
for doing this could consist of adding a couple&nbsp;
gnarlier landfall creatures such as Scute Swarm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.480 --> 00:08:37.480
or Moraug, and another option would be adding an&nbsp;
infinite combo of sort sort, but both of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.480 --> 00:08:42.600
approaches have problems. Adding the more powerful&nbsp;
landfall cards won’t improve the deck’s power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:47.320
level that much as these are still threats that&nbsp;
require some time and setup to turn into a win,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.320 --> 00:08:50.560
and the plan of adding a combo to our&nbsp;
control deck to help it win before an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.560 --> 00:08:55.160
opponent plays a threat it can’t deal with kinda&nbsp;
conflicts with its identity as a control deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.160 --> 00:09:01.000
right? Obviously this is subjective, but in my&nbsp;
book this deck’s gameplan should be to outgrind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:06.760
not outrun—generating resources and stabilizing&nbsp;
the boardstate should be your focus, not rushing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.760 --> 00:09:12.240
to find combo pieces. Overall, there are ways&nbsp;
for the deck to bump up its power level a bit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:19.520
but the most concrete one starts with buying or&nbsp;
proxying $180 worth of lands for a two color deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.520 --> 00:09:24.600
itself a move that feels strange, and further&nbsp;
upgrading attempts might risk fundamentally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:30.240
changing the identity of the deck in a way that is&nbsp;
less satisfying. Overall, I don’t think it’s worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:35.760
it to make major changes to this deck, and even&nbsp;
the handful of additions and cuts I would consider&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.760 --> 00:09:41.200
are subjective choices, and still maintain the&nbsp;
middling power level of the deck. This deck’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:46.520
conundrum, where upgrading is hard but playing&nbsp;
at mid-power can feel weird, is one I find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.520 --> 00:09:52.560
familiar— it reminds me of a scrapped deck idea I&nbsp;
featured in a pretty early video on this channel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.560 --> 00:09:58.560
A Child of Alara Maze’s End deck will likely&nbsp;
perform decently at a midrangy mid-power table,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.560 --> 00:10:03.040
but it’s a deck that will fall on its face in a&nbsp;
handful of matchups while creating a miserable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.040 --> 00:10:08.320
table experience in all of the other ones. I’m not&nbsp;
going to elaborate too much on that concept here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.320 --> 00:10:11.080
but I threw a link in the description&nbsp;
if you want to hear my full thoughts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.080 --> 00:10:15.360
on the subject of running a heavy-duty&nbsp;
removal suite at a middling power level.

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:20.480
Though the principle is the same, Gruul Control is&nbsp;
certainly less severe than the Child of Alara deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.480 --> 00:10:25.440
due to its removal being single target creature&nbsp;
removal rather than nonland permanent wipes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:30.240
and I actually think that fact gives it a valid&nbsp;
position at a lot of mid-power tables. As long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:34.880
as people know what the deck is capable of&nbsp;
and to expect to get their creatures yeeted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.880 --> 00:10:38.840
it can play into a flexible “archenemy”&nbsp;
dynamic, where the table tries to figure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.840 --> 00:10:43.520
out how much resources to spend suppressing it,&nbsp;
while also keeping in mind that fully locking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.520 --> 00:10:48.040
it out removes a roadblock for their opponents&nbsp;
to win with a creature-based wincon. This can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.040 --> 00:10:52.080
lead to some really cool gameplay—my Glissa&nbsp;
deck generally tends to play into a similar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.080 --> 00:10:56.960
dynamic—but it’s important to make sure that&nbsp;
the table is looking for that sort of gameplay.

00:10:56.960 --> 00:11:01.960
It’s also a type of deck that should be played&nbsp;
sparingly, in my opinion. It can be fun to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.960 --> 00:11:06.360
occasionally pull out a deck that demands the&nbsp;
table’s full attention, especially if the table&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.360 --> 00:11:10.840
is full of solitaire midrange, but pulling&nbsp;
out that type of deck too often can lead to other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.840 --> 00:11:15.960
people feeling like a side character in the four&nbsp;
player game they’re playing. This is the sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.960 --> 00:11:20.600
deck that’s fun to think and talk about to me&nbsp;
because it serves as an interesting case study&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.600 --> 00:11:26.320
into the ways people relate to games they play,&nbsp;
and what people find fun. That’s all I have to say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.320 --> 00:11:30.880
about it, but you can check out Jovism’s deck in&nbsp;
the description, and let me know if you think my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.880 --> 00:11:35.600
analysis is correct or if you have a different way&nbsp;
you’d go with the deck. Also make sure to do all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.600 --> 00:11:41.240
the interactive things, liking and subscribing and&nbsp;
all that jazz. I also wanna give a shout out to my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.240 --> 00:11:46.120
patrons, both in general for giving me money,&nbsp;
and for submitting decks for the drawing. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.120 --> 00:11:50.560
was a lot of fun, and I look forward to doing&nbsp;
more of these in the future. Alright, bye now.

